WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.731
Secure configuration in .

2
00:00:01.731 --> 00:00:04.569
NET is a repeatable engineering workflow.

3
00:00:04.569 --> 00:00:09.070
Start by classifying settings by sensitivity and security impact.

4
00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:12.116
Some settings are ordinary product behavior.

5
00:00:12.116 --> 00:00:21.393
Others affect identity, authorization, networking, storage, logging, data protection, diagnostics, cloud access, or deployment safety.

6
00:00:21.393 --> 00:00:26.586
Understand configuration sources, precedence, and effective runtime values.

7
00:00:26.586 --> 00:00:38.148
A setting may come from an appsettings file, an environment variable, a command-line argument, a secret store, a cloud configuration service, or a deployment pipeline.

8
00:00:38.148 --> 00:00:44.933
The running application only sees the final result after provider order and overrides are applied.

9
00:00:44.933 --> 00:00:48.533
Protect secrets and validate options before startup.

10
00:00:48.533 --> 00:01:00.026
Keep real secret values out of code and logs, use supported secret stores or managed identities, separate references from values, and validate strongly typed options.

11
00:01:00.026 --> 00:01:07.364
Unsafe or incomplete production configuration should fail clearly rather than silently weakening behavior.

12
00:01:07.364 --> 00:01:17.334
Review risky toggles, hosting settings, logging, diagnostics, CI/CD variables, cloud settings, runtime identities, and middleware configuration.

13
00:01:17.334 --> 00:01:21.280
Development convenience should not drift into production.

14
00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:25.988
Emergency settings should have ownership, expiration, and follow-up.

15
00:01:25.988 --> 00:01:28.896
Finally, maintain configuration over time.

16
00:01:28.896 --> 00:01:39.350
Monitor drift, review changes, rotate secrets, keep permissions narrow, document approved values, and connect operational feedback to engineering work.

17
00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:48.212
Secure configuration is not a static file; it is part of how the application stays trustworthy as environments and teams change.
